  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This eLife article is still early in its citation life, but it already shows a documented downstream reuse in Europe PMC. The record also places the paper in an unusually visible policy-and-practice conversation about how research is reviewed and published, while showing strong open-access availability across the article, PubMed, and PMC.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">\u201cRethinking how research is reviewed and published\u201d is a scholarly article about reforming the publication process itself. The abstract frames eLife\u2019s shift to a radical new review model as consequential enough to cost the journal its impact factor, yet it also notes continued support from authors, reviewers, editors, and funders for the reform effort. That makes the paper especially relevant to debates in scientific publishing, library science, research governance, and related fields.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">In the current record, the article has no citations in OpenAlex yet, so its scholarly citation impact is still too early to interpret. Even so, it already has one documented downstream reuse, registered as a Europe PMC article mention. That is a modest but concrete sign that the piece has entered the biomedical literature\u2019s referencing ecosystem.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The article\u2019s reach is also reinforced by its accessibility profile. It is gold open access and is available in multiple open instances, including the publisher site, PubMed, and PMC. This broad availability should make the work easier to read, reuse, and discuss across research and policy communities.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Other signals are limited or not yet populated in the record. There are no recorded scite citations or event-data mentions, and the teaching-adoption signal only confirms that the title is held in WorldCat rather than demonstrating course use. Overall, the evidence suggests an article with clear topical relevance and early reuse, but with scholarly citation impact that has not yet accumulated.</p>",
